{"name":"Aunt Violet","hex":"#7c0087","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aunt","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The sister or sister-in-law of one’s parent.","The female cousin or cousin-in-law of one’s parent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aunt"},{"term":"violet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant or flower of the genus Viola, especially the fragrant Viola odorata;  similar-looking plants and flowers.","A person thought to resemble V. odorata, especially in its beauty and delicacy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a violet colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/violet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aunt","blocks":[{"text":"An aunt is a female individual who is a sibling of a parent or married to a sibling of a parent. Aunts who are related by birth are second-degree relatives. Alternate terms include auntie or aunty."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Aunt_%26_Niece_%287936230820%29.jpg/330px-Aunt_%26_Niece_%287936230820%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aunt","wikidata":"Q76507"},{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violet_(color)","wikidata":"Q428124"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Aunt_%26_Niece_%287936230820%29.jpg/500px-Aunt_%26_Niece_%287936230820%29.jpg?utm_